---
title: SSH Tunnels
order: 4
description: -L local, -R remote, -D SOCKS, -J jump host, ProxyJump
tags: [ssh, tunnel, forward, proxy, socks, jump, bastion, port]
---

SSH tunnels encrypt traffic through an SSH connection. Local forwards
bring remote services to your machine; remote forwards expose local
services to the remote network; dynamic forwards create a SOCKS proxy.

@code bash
# ---- Local forward (-L): access remote service locally ----
# Syntax: -L [bind_addr:]local_port:target_host:target_port

# forward local:8080 -> remote_server:80 (through SSH host)
ssh -L 8080:localhost:80 user@sshhost
# now http://localhost:8080 reaches port 80 on sshhost

# access a service on the remote network
ssh -L 3306:dbserver:3306 user@sshhost
# now localhost:3306 reaches dbserver:3306 via sshhost

# bind to all interfaces (not just localhost)
ssh -L 0.0.0.0:8080:localhost:80 user@sshhost

# multiple forwards
ssh -L 8080:web:80 -L 3306:db:3306 user@sshhost

# background tunnel (no shell)
ssh -fNL 8080:localhost:80 user@sshhost
# -f  fork to background
# -N  no remote command

@code bash
# ---- Remote forward (-R): expose local service to remote ----
# Syntax: -R [bind_addr:]remote_port:target_host:target_port

# expose local web server on remote host
ssh -R 8080:localhost:3000 user@sshhost
# now sshhost:8080 reaches your localhost:3000

# expose to all interfaces on remote (requires GatewayPorts yes in sshd_config)
ssh -R 0.0.0.0:8080:localhost:3000 user@sshhost

# background
ssh -fNR 8080:localhost:3000 user@sshhost

@code bash
# ---- Dynamic forward (-D): SOCKS proxy ----
# Syntax: -D [bind_addr:]port

# create SOCKS5 proxy on local port 1080
ssh -D 1080 user@sshhost

# background SOCKS proxy
ssh -fND 1080 user@sshhost

# use with curl
curl --socks5 localhost:1080 https://example.com

# use with firefox: set SOCKS5 proxy to localhost:1080
# also set network.proxy.socks_remote_dns = true in about:config

# use with proxychains
# edit /etc/proxychains.conf: socks5 127.0.0.1 1080
proxychains curl https://example.com

@code bash
# ---- Jump hosts (-J / ProxyJump) ----

# connect through a bastion/jump host
ssh -J jumpuser@bastion user@internal

# multiple jumps (chained)
ssh -J jump1@bastion1,jump2@bastion2 user@internal

# with custom port on jump host
ssh -J jumpuser@bastion:2222 user@internal

# legacy method (ProxyCommand)
ssh -o ProxyCommand="ssh -W %h:%p jumpuser@bastion" user@internal

@text
ProxyJump in ~/.ssh/config is the cleanest approach for persistent
jump host configurations:

@code bash
# ~/.ssh/config

Host bastion
    HostName bastion.example.com
    User jumpuser
    Port 2222
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_bastion

Host internal-*
    ProxyJump bastion
    User admin
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_internal

Host internal-web
    HostName 10.0.0.10

Host internal-db
    HostName 10.0.0.20

# now just: ssh internal-web
# or: ssh internal-db

@code bash
# ---- tunnel management ----

# check if tunnel port is in use
ss -tlnp | grep 8080

# find and kill background tunnel
ps aux | grep 'ssh.*-[fN]'
kill <pid>

# autossh: persistent tunnel with auto-reconnect
# install: pacman -S autossh
autossh -M 0 -fNL 8080:localhost:80 user@sshhost
# -M 0 disables autossh monitoring port, uses SSH keepalive instead

# with keepalive (recommended with autossh -M 0)
autossh -M 0 -o "ServerAliveInterval 30" -o "ServerAliveCountMax 3" \
  -fNL 8080:localhost:80 user@sshhost

@text
Quick reference:

- `-L local:remote` -- bring remote to local (I want to access their stuff)
- `-R remote:local` -- push local to remote (they want to access my stuff)
- `-D port` -- SOCKS proxy (route all traffic through remote)
- `-J host` -- bounce through a jump host

Tunnel flags often combined with:
- `-f` -- fork to background after authentication
- `-N` -- no remote command (tunnel only)
- `-g` -- allow other hosts to use the forwarded port

@code bash
# ---- on-the-fly forwarding in active session ----

# press Enter then ~C to open SSH command line:
# ssh> -L 9090:localhost:9090     # add local forward
# ssh> -R 8080:localhost:80       # add remote forward
# ssh> -D 1080                    # add dynamic forward
# ssh> -KL 9090                   # cancel local forward on 9090
# ssh> -KR 8080                   # cancel remote forward on 8080
# ssh> -KD 1080                   # cancel dynamic forward
